Use ASP.NET Core's **`WebApplicationFactory<TEntryPoint>`** — it hosts your app in-memory and gives you an `HttpClient` wired to it. No port, no real network.

### 1. Add the test package

```bash
dotnet add tests/MyMcp.Tests.csproj package Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing
```

### 2. Create a fixture

```csharp
public class McpFixture : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public McpFixture(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        // CreateClient() talks to the in-memory TestServer
        _client = factory.CreateClient();
    }
}
```

> Make sure your `Program` class is accessible (add `public partial class Program { }` to the bottom of `Program.cs` if using top-level statements).

### 3. Write an MCP protocol test

MCP over HTTP uses **SSE** (`GET /sse`) for the server→client channel and **JSON-RPC POSTs** for client→server. A typical test:

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task Initialize_ReturnsCapabilities()
{
    // 1. Connect to the SSE endpoint to get the message endpoint URI
    var sseResponse = await _client.GetAsync("/sse",
        HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead);
    sseResponse.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

    using var stream = await sseResponse.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
    using var reader = new StreamReader(stream);

    // 2. Read the first SSE event — it contains the POST endpoint
    var postEndpoint = await ReadSseEndpoint(reader);

    // 3. Send an MCP "initialize" JSON-RPC request
    var initRequest = new
    {
        jsonrpc = "2.0",
        id = 1,
        method = "initialize",
        @params = new
        {
            protocolVersion = "2025-03-26",
            clientInfo = new { name = "test", version = "1.0" },
            capabilities = new { }
        }
    };

    var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync(postEndpoint, initRequest);
    response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

    // 4. Read the SSE event carrying the JSON-RPC response
    var (eventType, data) = await ReadNextSseEvent(reader);
    Assert.Equal("message", eventType);

    using var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(data);
    var root = doc.RootElement;
    Assert.Equal(1, root.GetProperty("id").GetInt32());
    Assert.True(root.TryGetProperty("result", out var result));
    Assert.Equal("2025-03-26",
        result.GetProperty("protocolVersion").GetString());
}
```

### 4. SSE helper methods

```csharp
private static async Task<string> ReadSseEndpoint(StreamReader reader)
{
    // Reads "event: endpoint\ndata: /messages?sessionId=...\n\n"
    string? eventLine, dataLine;
    do { eventLine = await reader.ReadLineAsync(); }
    while (eventLine != null && !eventLine.StartsWith("event:"));

    dataLine = await reader.ReadLineAsync(); // "data: ..."
    await reader.ReadLineAsync();            // blank line
    return dataLine!.Substring("data: ".Length).Trim();
}

private static async Task<(string type, string data)>
    ReadNextSseEvent(StreamReader reader)
{
    string? line;
    string type = "", data = "";
    while ((line = await reader.ReadLineAsync()) != null)
    {
        if (line.StartsWith("event: ")) type = line[7..];
        else if (line.StartsWith("data: ")) data = line[6..];
        else if (line == "") break; // end of event
    }
    return (type, data);
}
```

### Key points

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **No real server** | `WebApplicationFactory` hosts in-process via `TestServer` |
| **Full protocol fidelity** | Real HTTP, real SSE streaming, real JSON-RPC |
| **Custom config** | Override `WithWebHostBuilder(b => b.ConfigureServices(...))` to swap dependencies |
| **Timeouts** | Use `CancellationTokenSource` with a timeout on SSE reads to avoid hanging tests |

This gives you true end-to-end MCP coverage (initialize → tool calls → notifications) entirely in-process.
